window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["apache-spark"]=[{"id":"apache-spark-f0001","card":"q-apache-spark-001-1-data-engineering-concepts","sec":"apache-spark-s001","q":"What is data engineering, and what problem does it solve for analytics and machine learning teams?","a":"<p>Data engineering designs and runs the systems that collect, move, clean, transform and store data at scale, so analysts and ML models can use it. Raw data from applications is messy, scattered and too large for one machine. Without this layer, analysts spend their time fixing data and ML models train on bad inputs. Inference also needs pre-processing and post-processing, so the work does not stop after training.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0002","card":"q-apache-spark-001-1-data-engineering-concepts","sec":"apache-spark-s001","q":"Why is data engineering called a prerequisite for machine learning?","a":"<p>Training data must be cleansed, joined and transformed into features before a model can learn from it. The same transformations must run again at inference time, usually at low latency. A model trained on inconsistent or stale data gives unreliable predictions, whatever the algorithm.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0003","card":"q-apache-spark-002-data-engineering-vs-analytics-vs-data-sc","sec":"apache-spark-s002","q":"How do data engineering, business analytics and data science differ in scope?","a":"<p>Data engineering prepares data: integration, pipelines, transport, transformation, aggregation and storage. Business analytics works on prepared data for dashboards, reports, exploration and statistical modeling. Data science covers both and adds machine learning and prediction. In practice a data scientist depends on the engineer's pipelines, and an analyst depends on their output tables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0004","card":"q-apache-spark-002-data-engineering-vs-analytics-vs-data-sc","sec":"apache-spark-s002","q":"A team has raw event logs and wants a daily sales dashboard. Which role builds which part?","a":"<p>The data engineer builds the pipeline that ingests the logs, cleans them and writes aggregated tables to a store. The analyst builds the dashboard and queries on those tables. A data scientist is only needed if the goal moves to prediction, such as a sales forecast.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0005","card":"q-apache-spark-003-the-five-data-engineering-functions","sec":"apache-spark-s003","q":"What are the five functions of a data engineering pipeline, in order?","a":"<p>Acquisition (extract or receive data from upstream systems), transport (move it to processing or storage), storage (keep it for later use), processing (cleanse, enrich, aggregate) and serving (expose it to consumers). Each stage has its own scale, reliability, latency and security needs, and the whole pipeline is only as strong as its weakest stage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0006","card":"q-apache-spark-003-the-five-data-engineering-functions","sec":"apache-spark-s003","q":"In the acquisition stage, what is the difference between extracting data and exposing an interface for upstream systems to push to?","a":"<p>Extracting means your pipeline pulls from the upstream system, for example by querying a database or reading files. It is simple to control but loads the upstream system and has polling latency. Exposing an interface (an API or a message topic) lets producers push events, which gives lower latency and decouples the two sides, but you must handle security, back-pressure and reliability of that endpoint.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0007","card":"q-apache-spark-003-the-five-data-engineering-functions","sec":"apache-spark-s003","q":"What should you weigh when choosing storage for a pipeline's output?","a":"<p>Schema flexibility, the query patterns consumers will use, availability and redundancy, and cost. A data lake of columnar files suits large scans and flexible schemas. A relational database suits point lookups and transactional reads. A key-value store suits fast lookups by key. Pick for how the data will be read, not for how it arrives.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0008","card":"q-apache-spark-003-the-five-data-engineering-functions","sec":"apache-spark-s003","q":"Why does the serving stage consider consumer skill levels and not only technology?","a":"<p>Consumers differ: analysts want SQL tables or a BI connection, applications want a low-latency API, and data scientists want files or feature tables. The same data may need to be served in several shapes. If the interface does not match the consumer, the data is not used however good the pipeline is.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0009","card":"q-apache-spark-004-batch-vs-real-time-stream-processing","sec":"apache-spark-s004","q":"How do batch and stream processing differ in input, latency and results?","a":"<p>Batch works on a bounded input that does not change during the run, has higher latency (it waits for the batch), and produces results at the end. Stream processing works on an unbounded flow of records as they arrive, has latency in seconds or less, and produces results continuously. Batch is simpler. Streaming is more complex because of state, late data and changing input.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0010","card":"q-apache-spark-004-batch-vs-real-time-stream-processing","sec":"apache-spark-s004","q":"Why is state management central to stream processing but usually minor in batch?","a":"<p>A batch job sees the whole dataset, so it can compute totals in one pass and keep no memory between runs. A stream never ends, so a running total, a user session or a window of events must be held between records and survive failures. That state must be stored, bounded in size, and recovered after a restart.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0011","card":"q-apache-spark-004-batch-vs-real-time-stream-processing","sec":"apache-spark-s004","q":"Why can you rerun a batch job safely but not simply rerun a streaming job to reprocess data?","a":"<p>A batch job with the same input gives the same output, so a rerun is easy as long as the writes are idempotent or the target is cleaned first. A streaming job carries checkpoints, state and consumed offsets. To reprocess, you must reset or delete those and decide how the sink handles records it already received, otherwise you get gaps or double counts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0012","card":"q-apache-spark-005-why-spark-for-data-engineering","sec":"apache-spark-s005","q":"Why would you choose Apache Spark for a data engineering platform?","a":"<p>One engine handles both batch and streaming, with built-in connectors for JDBC databases, Kafka and HDFS or object storage. It processes data in parallel across a cluster, supports MapReduce-style transforms, joins, windowing and state, and has SQL and machine learning libraries in the same program. That reduces the number of separate tools to run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0013","card":"q-apache-spark-005-why-spark-for-data-engineering","sec":"apache-spark-s005","q":"What is the trade-off of using Spark for a small, low-latency job?","a":"<p>Spark has start-up and scheduling overhead and needs a JVM and cluster or local runtime. For small data a single-process tool such as pandas or a database query is faster and simpler. For true per-record millisecond latency, a record-at-a-time engine such as Apache Flink or Kafka Streams may fit better, because Spark Structured Streaming works in micro-batches by default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0014","card":"q-apache-spark-005-why-spark-for-data-engineering","sec":"apache-spark-s005","q":"How does Spark compare with Flink for stream processing?","a":"<p>Spark Structured Streaming defaults to micro-batches, which gives high throughput and one API for batch and streaming, with latency typically around 100 ms to seconds. Flink processes record by record with native event-time and state handling, which suits very low latency and complex event processing. Choose Spark if you also need batch, SQL and ML in one stack. Consider Flink for strict low latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0015","card":"q-apache-spark-006-architecture-and-execution-flow","sec":"apache-spark-s006","q":"What are the roles of the driver and the executors in a Spark application?","a":"<p>The driver runs your program, builds the execution plan, and schedules tasks. Executors are processes on worker nodes that run those tasks on data partitions and hold cached data. A cluster manager (standalone, YARN or Kubernetes) provides the executors. If the driver dies, the application dies, so its memory and placement matter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0016","card":"q-apache-spark-006-architecture-and-execution-flow","sec":"apache-spark-s006","q":"Walk through how Spark executes a typical pipeline from read to write.","a":"<p>Spark reads the input into a DataFrame or RDD split into partitions spread over executors. Narrow transformations such as map and filter run locally on each partition with no data movement. Wide operations such as groupBy, join and reduce shuffle data between executors. Results are written by the executors in parallel, or collected to the driver if you call collect. Work is organized as jobs, split into stages at shuffle boundaries, and each stage into one task per partition.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0017","card":"q-apache-spark-006-architecture-and-execution-flow","sec":"apache-spark-s006","q":"Which Spark operations cause a shuffle, and why is a shuffle costly?","a":"<p>Wide operations such as <code>groupBy</code>, <code>join</code>, <code>distinct</code>, <code>repartition</code> and <code>orderBy</code> shuffle. Rows must move across the network to executors that hold their key, which means disk writes, network transfer, serialization and memory pressure. A shuffle also ends a stage, so it is usually the main bottleneck. Map and filter do not shuffle.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0018","card":"q-apache-spark-006-architecture-and-execution-flow","sec":"apache-spark-s006","q":"Why is <code>collect()</code> risky in a Spark job, and what do you use instead?","a":"<p><code>collect()</code> pulls every partition into the driver's memory, so a large result causes an out-of-memory error and removes the parallelism. Use <code>take(n)</code> or <code>limit(n).show()</code> to inspect, and write large results to storage from the executors with <code>df.write</code>. Collect only small final results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0019","card":"q-apache-spark-006-architecture-and-execution-flow","sec":"apache-spark-s006","q":"Why should you draw the flow of each pipeline before tuning it?","a":"<p>Pipeline speed is set by its slowest stage. Marking where data is read, where shuffles happen and where results are written shows the likely bottlenecks, such as an unpartitioned read, a large shuffle or a driver collect. You then fix the biggest one instead of guessing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0020","card":"q-apache-spark-007-parallel-reads-processing-writes","sec":"apache-spark-s007","q":"How does Spark read from a JDBC database in parallel, and what happens if you do not configure it?","a":"<p>With <code>partitionColumn</code>, <code>lowerBound</code>, <code>upperBound</code> and <code>numPartitions</code>, Spark splits the range into slices and each task reads one slice with its own query. Without them, Spark reads the whole table through one connection in one task, so the read is not parallel whatever the cluster size.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0021","card":"q-apache-spark-007-parallel-reads-processing-writes","sec":"apache-spark-s007","q":"In a Spark JDBC read, do <code>lowerBound</code> and <code>upperBound</code> filter rows?","a":"<p>No. They only decide the stride of each partition. Rows outside the range still get read, falling into the first or last partition, which can create skew. Filtering must be done in the query itself. The partition column must be numeric, date or timestamp.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0022","card":"q-apache-spark-007-parallel-reads-processing-writes","sec":"apache-spark-s007","q":"Which column makes a good <code>partitionColumn</code> for a JDBC read?","a":"<p>An indexed numeric, date or timestamp column with evenly spread values, such as an auto-increment ID. A skewed column leaves one task with most of the rows. An unindexed column makes each slice query scan the whole table.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0023","card":"q-apache-spark-007-parallel-reads-processing-writes","sec":"apache-spark-s007","q":"How should you choose <code>numPartitions</code> for a JDBC read or write?","a":"<p>It sets both the number of parallel tasks and the maximum number of simultaneous database connections. Choose it by the database's capacity and the cluster's cores. Too high can overload the database with connections or locks, and too low leaves the cluster idle.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0024","card":"q-apache-spark-007-parallel-reads-processing-writes","sec":"apache-spark-s007","q":"How does Spark read a Kafka topic in parallel?","a":"<p>Kafka partitions are divided among Spark tasks, so each executor reads a subset of partitions. The partition count of the topic therefore caps source parallelism. A one-partition topic is read by one task. The Kafka source <code>minPartitions</code> option can split partitions further.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0025","card":"q-apache-spark-007-parallel-reads-processing-writes","sec":"apache-spark-s007","q":"What is predicate pushdown, and where does it help?","a":"<p>The optimizer sends filter conditions to the data source so unneeded rows are never read or transferred. With a JDBC database the filter becomes part of the SQL query, and with Parquet it lets Spark skip row groups using min and max statistics. This reduces I/O and memory use, and Spark applies it automatically when the source and the expression support it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0026","card":"q-apache-spark-007-parallel-reads-processing-writes","sec":"apache-spark-s007","q":"Why filter and map early and reduce late in a Spark pipeline?","a":"<p>Filters and maps are local to each partition and fully parallel, and filtering early shrinks the data before any shuffle. A reduce or group-by shuffles data, so doing it on less data moves fewer bytes. Late reduces and early filters give the lowest I/O and memory use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0027","card":"q-apache-spark-007-parallel-reads-processing-writes","sec":"apache-spark-s007","q":"How do writes to a JDBC database scale, and what can slow them down?","a":"<p>Each executor writes its partitions over its own connection, in parallel. The number of write partitions equals the number of connections, so repartition or coalesce to a number the database can handle. Row-by-row inserts are slow, so use the JDBC <code>batchsize</code> option to batch statements and cut network round trips.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0028","card":"q-apache-spark-008-execution-plan-lazy-evaluation","sec":"apache-spark-s008","q":"What is lazy evaluation in Spark, and what benefit does it give?","a":"<p>Transformations such as <code>select</code>, <code>filter</code> and <code>join</code> only build a plan. Nothing runs until an action such as <code>count</code>, <code>collect</code>, <code>show</code> or a write is called. Spark then sees the whole chain and the optimizer can reorder and combine steps, push filters to the source, prune unused columns and reduce shuffles.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0029","card":"q-apache-spark-008-execution-plan-lazy-evaluation","sec":"apache-spark-s008","q":"In Spark, what is the difference between a transformation and an action? Give examples.","a":"<p>A transformation returns a new DataFrame or RDD and is lazy: <code>select</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>groupBy().agg()</code>, <code>join</code>. An action triggers execution and returns a result or writes output: <code>count</code>, <code>collect</code>, <code>show</code>, <code>take</code>, <code>write</code>. Each action starts a job.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0030","card":"q-apache-spark-008-execution-plan-lazy-evaluation","sec":"apache-spark-s008","q":"How do you look at the execution plan Spark builds for a query, and what do you look for?","a":"<p>Call <code>df.explain()</code>, or <code>df.explain(True)</code> for the logical plans too. In Spark 3.0 and later, <code>df.explain(\"formatted\")</code> gives a readable layout. Look for <code>Exchange</code> nodes (shuffles), the <code>PushedFilters</code> entry on the scan, whether the join is a broadcast join, and unneeded columns being read.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0031","card":"q-apache-spark-008-execution-plan-lazy-evaluation","sec":"apache-spark-s008","q":"A teammate says \"my code ran with no error but nothing happened\". What is the likely cause in Spark?","a":"<p>Only transformations were defined, and no action was called, so Spark never executed anything. Add an action or write step. The flip side is that an error in a transformation often appears only when the action runs, which makes debugging harder.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0032","card":"q-apache-spark-008-execution-plan-lazy-evaluation","sec":"apache-spark-s008","q":"Why does Spark's lazy planning help when the source is a database?","a":"<p>Spark sees the filter and the selected columns before it reads, so it can push them into the database query. Rows and columns that are not needed never leave the database, and executors can read slices in parallel instead of the driver loading everything.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0033","card":"q-apache-spark-009-stateful-stream-processing","sec":"apache-spark-s009","q":"What does checkpointing do in a Spark streaming job, and where should the checkpoint be stored?","a":"<p>A checkpoint saves progress and state, such as source offsets, the query's metadata and aggregation state, so a restarted job resumes where it stopped instead of losing or repeating work. Store it on a reliable shared file system such as HDFS or S3, not a local disk that disappears with the node.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0034","card":"q-apache-spark-009-stateful-stream-processing","sec":"apache-spark-s009","q":"In Structured Streaming, how do you enable checkpointing, and what happens if you change the query a lot between restarts?","a":"<p>Set the <code>checkpointLocation</code> option on <code>writeStream</code>. On restart Spark reads offsets and state from it. Some query changes are compatible, but changing the aggregation, the grouping keys or the number of state partitions can make the old state unusable, and you must start with a new checkpoint directory and reprocess.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0035","card":"q-apache-spark-009-stateful-stream-processing","sec":"apache-spark-s009","q":"What is a watermark in stream processing and why is it needed?","a":"<p>A watermark is the allowed lateness for event-time data. Spark tracks the maximum event time seen, and treats the watermark as that value minus the delay threshold. Windows older than the watermark are finalized and their state is dropped, and later events for them are discarded. Without a watermark, state for every window would grow forever.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0036","card":"q-apache-spark-009-stateful-stream-processing","sec":"apache-spark-s009","q":"What is the difference between event time and processing time, and which should windows use?","a":"<p>Event time is when the event happened at the origin, and processing time is when Spark handles it. Windows over event time give correct results despite network delay and out-of-order arrival, but need a watermark to bound state. Processing-time windows are simpler but results depend on arrival timing and are not reproducible on replay.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0037","card":"q-apache-spark-009-stateful-stream-processing","sec":"apache-spark-s009","q":"What is the trade-off when you set a longer watermark delay?","a":"<p>A longer delay accepts more late events and gives more accurate results, but windows are finalized later, so output in append mode is delayed and Spark must keep more state in memory. A shorter delay lowers latency and state but drops more late data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0038","card":"q-apache-spark-009-stateful-stream-processing","sec":"apache-spark-s009","q":"How do you track per-entity state, such as a user session, in Spark streaming?","a":"<p>In the legacy DStream API you use <code>updateStateByKey</code>, which keeps a state per key and updates it with new events. In Structured Streaming you use <code>mapGroupsWithState</code> or <code>flatMapGroupsWithState</code> (Scala and Java) and, in PySpark 3.4 and later, <code>applyInPandasWithState</code>. These keep state per key in the checkpointed state store, with timeouts to expire idle keys.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0039","card":"q-apache-spark-009-stateful-stream-processing","sec":"apache-spark-s009","q":"Why prefer Structured Streaming to the DStream API for new projects?","a":"<p>DStreams (Spark Streaming) are a legacy API with RDD-level code, processing-time semantics and no query optimizer. Structured Streaming uses DataFrames and SQL, supports event time and watermarks, and goes through the Catalyst optimizer. The Spark project directs new streaming development to Structured Streaming.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0040","card":"q-apache-spark-009-stateful-stream-processing","sec":"apache-spark-s009","q":"What can go wrong with watermarks and windows in a streaming aggregation?","a":"<p>A watermark that is too short drops late events and gives wrong totals. A watermark with no event-time column, or one based on processing time, does not protect against out-of-order data. Too-long delays raise memory use and latency. Test with late and out-of-order data before trusting the numbers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0041","card":"q-apache-spark-010-spark-sql-and-ml","sec":"apache-spark-s010","q":"How does Spark SQL relate to the DataFrame API, and when would you use each?","a":"<p>Both go through the same Catalyst optimizer and produce the same kind of plan, so performance is equivalent. SQL suits analysts and set-based logic, and it is easy to read. The DataFrame API suits programmatic, composable code with compile-time or IDE support. They can be mixed in one pipeline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0042","card":"q-apache-spark-010-spark-sql-and-ml","sec":"apache-spark-s010","q":"In PySpark, what does <code>df.createOrReplaceTempView(\"t\")</code> do and why use it?","a":"<p>It registers the DataFrame as a temporary view named <code>t</code> in the current Spark session, so you can query it with <code>spark.sql(\"SELECT ... FROM t\")</code>. It does not copy or save data and disappears when the session ends. It is the bridge from DataFrame code to SQL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0043","card":"q-apache-spark-010-spark-sql-and-ml","sec":"apache-spark-s010","q":"Why is it useful that Spark SQL works on both batch and streaming data?","a":"<p>The same SQL logic can run on a static table or on a streaming DataFrame, and the output of one query can feed the next in a cascade. Teams reuse business logic between the nightly batch and the live pipeline and test it on static data first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0044","card":"q-apache-spark-010-spark-sql-and-ml","sec":"apache-spark-s010","q":"What does a Spark ML pipeline give you over doing the feature steps by hand?","a":"<p>A <code>Pipeline</code> chains stages such as feature extraction, transformers and an estimator into one object. Fitting it trains all stages in order, and the fitted pipeline model applies exactly the same transformations at inference. That avoids training and serving drift and lets you save and load the whole workflow.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0045","card":"q-apache-spark-010-spark-sql-and-ml","sec":"apache-spark-s010","q":"In Spark ML, what is the difference between a Transformer and an Estimator?","a":"<p>A Transformer converts one DataFrame to another with <code>transform</code>, for example a fitted model or a tokenizer. An Estimator has a <code>fit</code> method that learns from data and returns a Transformer. For example, a <code>StandardScaler</code> estimator learns means and variances and returns a scaler model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0046","card":"q-apache-spark-010-spark-sql-and-ml","sec":"apache-spark-s010","q":"Which Spark machine learning library should you use: <code>spark.ml</code> or <code>spark.mllib</code>?","a":"<p>Use <code>spark.ml</code>, which is DataFrame-based and holds the active development. The RDD-based <code>spark.mllib</code> API is in maintenance mode and receives only bug fixes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0047","card":"q-apache-spark-011-3-environment-setup","sec":"apache-spark-s011","q":"Why would you run Kafka, a database and Redis as Docker containers for local pipeline development?","a":"<p>Containers give a repeatable, isolated setup with one command and no installs on the machine. Everyone gets the same versions, and you can destroy and recreate the environment. The cost is that data is lost when a container is removed unless you mount a volume.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0048","card":"q-apache-spark-011-3-environment-setup","sec":"apache-spark-s011","q":"What do <code>docker-compose -f &lt;file&gt; up -d</code> and <code>docker ps</code> do?","a":"<p><code>up -d</code> creates and starts all services in the Compose file in detached (background) mode. <code>-f</code> selects the Compose file. <code>docker ps</code> lists running containers so you can confirm that all services are up.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0049","card":"q-apache-spark-011-3-environment-setup","sec":"apache-spark-s011","q":"A database container loses its tables and data after you tear down and recreate it. How do you prevent that?","a":"<p>Mount a named volume or a host directory at the database's data directory in the Compose file, such as <code>/var/lib/mysql</code> for MariaDB. Without a volume, data lives in the container's writable layer and disappears when the container is removed. Note that <code>docker-compose down -v</code> also deletes named volumes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0050","card":"q-apache-spark-011-3-environment-setup","sec":"apache-spark-s011","q":"In a Docker Compose file, what does <code>ports: - '3306:3306'</code> mean?","a":"<p>It publishes container port 3306 on host port 3306, in the order host:container. It lets tools on your machine, such as a Spark job in a notebook, connect to the database at <code>localhost:3306</code>. Containers on the same Compose network reach each other by service name and do not need the published port.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0051","card":"q-apache-spark-011-3-environment-setup","sec":"apache-spark-s011","q":"In Kafka, why are <code>ADVERTISED_LISTENERS</code> needed, and why can you set two listeners such as one for <code>localhost</code> and one for the container network?","a":"<p>A Kafka client connects to the bootstrap address, and then the broker tells it which address to use for each partition leader. That is the advertised address. If it is not reachable from the client, connection fails after the first call. Clients on the host need <code>localhost:9092</code>, while containers on the Docker network need a service-name address, so you define separate listeners for each.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0052","card":"q-apache-spark-011-3-environment-setup","sec":"apache-spark-s011","q":"What is Kafka KRaft mode, and what does it remove?","a":"<p>KRaft lets Kafka manage its own metadata through a Raft-based controller quorum, so it needs no ZooKeeper. A node can have process roles <code>broker</code>, <code>controller</code> or both. KRaft is production ready from Kafka 3.3, and Kafka 4.0 removed ZooKeeper mode.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0053","card":"q-apache-spark-011-3-environment-setup","sec":"apache-spark-s011","q":"Why must a single-broker development Kafka use replication factor 1?","a":"<p>A partition's replicas must live on different brokers, so the replication factor cannot exceed the number of brokers. With one broker the factor is 1, which means no fault tolerance. Production should use at least 3 brokers with replication factor 3.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0054","card":"q-apache-spark-012-3-environment-setup","sec":"apache-spark-s011","q":"Why create a separate conda environment for a PySpark project?","a":"<p>It isolates the Python version and package versions from other projects, so a pinned <code>pyspark</code> release does not clash with other libraries. It also gives repeatable setups. <code>conda create -n spark python=3.11</code> makes the environment and <code>conda activate spark</code> switches to it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0055","card":"q-apache-spark-012-3-environment-setup","sec":"apache-spark-s011","q":"A PySpark job fails to start with a Java error or \"python not found\". Which environment variables do you check?","a":"<p><code>JAVA_HOME</code> must point to a Java version that your Spark release supports (Spark 3.5 supports Java 8, 11 and 17; Spark 4.0 supports 17 and 21). <code>PYSPARK_PYTHON</code> (and <code>PYSPARK_DRIVER_PYTHON</code>) must point to the Python interpreter that has <code>pyspark</code> installed, and the driver and workers must use the same Python minor version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0056","card":"q-apache-spark-012-3-environment-setup","sec":"apache-spark-s011","q":"Why do people pin library versions such as <code>pyspark==3.5.1</code> in a setup?","a":"<p>Spark, its connector jars, and Python libraries only work in tested combinations. A Kafka connector jar must match the Spark and Scala version, for example. Pinning keeps runs repeatable and avoids breakage from silent upgrades.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0057","card":"q-apache-spark-012-3-environment-setup","sec":"apache-spark-s011","q":"What extra step does PySpark need on Windows, and why?","a":"<p>Spark's Hadoop file system layer expects native Windows binaries (<code>winutils.exe</code> and related files), so you place a Hadoop folder locally, set <code>HADOOP_HOME</code> to it, add its <code>bin</code> folder to <code>PATH</code>, and restart the terminal or notebook so the variables are picked up.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0058","card":"q-apache-spark-012-3-environment-setup","sec":"apache-spark-s011","q":"What does <code>findspark</code> do, and do you still need it with <code>pip install pyspark</code>?","a":"<p><code>findspark.init()</code> adds an existing Spark installation to <code>sys.path</code> so you can import <code>pyspark</code>. If you installed PySpark with pip, the package is already importable and you do not need it. It matters when using a separate Spark download.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0059","card":"q-apache-spark-012-3-environment-setup","sec":"apache-spark-s011","q":"Does a local PySpark install need a cluster?","a":"<p>No. The pip package includes Spark, and with <code>master(\"local[N]\")</code> the driver and executors run as threads in one JVM on your machine. It is good for development and tests but gives no real distribution, so cluster issues like serialization across nodes and shuffle over network do not appear.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0060","card":"q-apache-spark-013-3-environment-setup","sec":"apache-spark-s011","q":"Why does a setup script use \"check if the database exists, then create it\" instead of just running <code>CREATE DATABASE</code>?","a":"<p>Re-running the script then does not fail or wipe data. Plain <code>CREATE DATABASE</code> errors if the database exists, and dropping and recreating destroys data. A check first (or <code>CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS</code>) makes the setup idempotent, so it is safe to run repeatedly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0061","card":"q-apache-spark-013-3-environment-setup","sec":"apache-spark-s011","q":"Why does an application user get its own <code>GRANT</code> on one database instead of using the MariaDB root user?","a":"<p>Least privilege. The application only needs rights on its own database, so a bug or leaked credential cannot touch other databases or administer the server. Root is used only for setup. Never hard-code real passwords, and read them from environment variables or a secret store.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0062","card":"q-apache-spark-013-3-environment-setup","sec":"apache-spark-s011","q":"In MariaDB, is <code>FLUSH PRIVILEGES</code> needed after a <code>GRANT</code> statement?","a":"<p>No. <code>GRANT</code>, <code>REVOKE</code> and <code>CREATE USER</code> update the privilege tables and the in-memory copy together. <code>FLUSH PRIVILEGES</code> is only needed after you edit the grant tables directly with <code>INSERT</code> or <code>UPDATE</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0063","card":"q-apache-spark-013-3-environment-setup","sec":"apache-spark-s011","q":"What do <code>AUTO_INCREMENT</code> and an index on the date column provide in a table that Spark will read?","a":"<p><code>AUTO_INCREMENT</code> gives a unique, increasing numeric ID that works as a parallel-read <code>partitionColumn</code> with even slices. The index on the date column makes the date-range filters in the Spark query fast, so each parallel slice does not scan the whole table.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0064","card":"q-apache-spark-013-3-environment-setup","sec":"apache-spark-s011","q":"How do you create Kafka topics from Python, and what do <code>num_partitions</code> and <code>replication_factor</code> control?","a":"<p>Use <code>kafka.admin.KafkaAdminClient</code> with <code>NewTopic</code> objects and <code>create_topics</code>. <code>num_partitions</code> sets how many partitions the topic has, which caps consumer parallelism and ordering domains. <code>replication_factor</code> sets how many brokers keep a copy of each partition, and it cannot exceed the broker count.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0065","card":"q-apache-spark-014-4-batch-pipeline-warehouse-stock-aggrega","sec":"apache-spark-s012","q":"How would you design a daily pipeline that consolidates data from many regional databases into one central database using Spark?","a":"<p>Each region runs a job that reads its local database and writes Parquet files to a central file system (HDFS or object storage), partitioned by date and region. After the uploads finish, a central Spark job reads all the Parquet files, aggregates with Spark SQL and writes the summary to the central database. The files are pruned after processing. Splitting extract and aggregate lets each scale on its own and lets you rerun aggregation without touching the regional databases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0066","card":"q-apache-spark-014-4-batch-pipeline-warehouse-stock-aggrega","sec":"apache-spark-s012","q":"Why must the aggregation job start only after all uploads have finished?","a":"<p>Aggregation over a partial set of files gives wrong totals. The job needs a completion signal, such as an orchestrator dependency (Airflow task order, for example) or a marker file. Spark itself does not know that other jobs are still uploading.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0067","card":"q-apache-spark-014-4-batch-pipeline-warehouse-stock-aggrega","sec":"apache-spark-s012","q":"Why must a multi-node Spark pipeline write its Parquet output to S3 or HDFS instead of a local path such as <code>/data/stock/</code>?","a":"<p>A local path exists only on one machine, and executors on other nodes cannot read it. Use a shared distributed store such as <code>s3a://bucket/path</code> or <code>hdfs://...</code> so all executors read and write the same location.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0068","card":"q-apache-spark-015-4-1-local-db-connection-and-sample-data","sec":"apache-spark-s013","q":"In a Python script, why is building SQL with an f-string a problem, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>Putting values directly into the SQL text allows SQL injection and breaks on quotes in values. Use parameterized queries and pass the values separately. With the MariaDB connector the placeholder is <code>?</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cur.execute(\n    &quot;INSERT INTO item_stock (item_name, receipts) VALUES (?, ?)&quot;,\n    (name, receipts),\n)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0069","card":"q-apache-spark-015-4-1-local-db-connection-and-sample-data","sec":"apache-spark-s013","q":"What does <code>autocommit=True</code> do on a MariaDB connection in Python, and when would you turn it off?","a":"<p>With autocommit on, each statement is committed immediately. That is convenient for single inserts and DDL. Turn it off when several statements must succeed or fail together, then call <code>commit()</code> or <code>rollback()</code> yourself. It also matters for bulk inserts, where one commit at the end is much faster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0070","card":"q-apache-spark-015-4-1-local-db-connection-and-sample-data","sec":"apache-spark-s013","q":"Why use <code>executemany</code> or batched inserts instead of one <code>execute</code> per row when loading many rows into MariaDB?","a":"<p>Per-row statements cost a network round trip each. <code>executemany</code> sends them in batches, which cuts the latency a lot. For very large loads, use bulk tools such as <code>LOAD DATA</code> or write from Spark with JDBC batching.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0071","card":"q-apache-spark-016-4-2-upload-stock-to-the-central-store-pa","sec":"apache-spark-s014","q":"What does the <code>.master(\"local[2]\")</code> setting do when you build a <code>SparkSession</code>, and how does it differ from <code>local[*]</code>?","a":"<p>It runs Spark locally with two worker threads, so at most two tasks run at once. <code>local[*]</code> uses as many threads as the machine has cores, and <code>local</code> uses one thread. In a cluster deployment you normally do not hard-code <code>master</code> in code, and you pass it to <code>spark-submit</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0072","card":"q-apache-spark-016-4-2-upload-stock-to-the-central-store-pa","sec":"apache-spark-s014","q":"In a <code>SparkSession</code>, what do <code>spark.sql.shuffle.partitions</code> and <code>spark.default.parallelism</code> each control?","a":"<p><code>spark.sql.shuffle.partitions</code> is the number of partitions after a shuffle in DataFrame and SQL operations such as joins and aggregations. Its default is 200. <code>spark.default.parallelism</code> is the default partition count for RDD operations such as <code>reduceByKey</code> and <code>parallelize</code>. Lower values suit small local data, and larger values suit big data on big clusters. Since Spark 3.2, adaptive query execution is on by default and can coalesce shuffle partitions automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0073","card":"q-apache-spark-016-4-2-upload-stock-to-the-central-store-pa","sec":"apache-spark-s014","q":"What is <code>getOrCreate()</code> in <code>SparkSession.builder</code>, and what is the gotcha with configs?","a":"<p>It returns the existing session if one is already active in the process, or builds a new one. If a session exists, new static configs in the builder are ignored, such as jar or memory settings. Restart the kernel or stop the session to apply changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0074","card":"q-apache-spark-016-4-2-upload-stock-to-the-central-store-pa","sec":"apache-spark-s014","q":"How do you make Spark read a JDBC database through a driver jar, and what are the ways to supply it?","a":"<p>The JDBC driver must be on the classpath of the driver and the executors. Options are <code>--jars</code> or <code>spark.jars</code> with a jar path, <code>spark.jars.packages</code> with a Maven coordinate (downloaded at start-up), or <code>spark.driver.extraClassPath</code> and <code>spark.executor.extraClassPath</code> for jars already on each node. For a cluster, <code>--packages</code> or a jar path shipped with the job is simplest.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0075","card":"q-apache-spark-016-4-2-upload-stock-to-the-central-store-pa","sec":"apache-spark-s014","q":"How do you fetch a subset of a table in a Spark JDBC read, and why wrap the query in parentheses with an alias?","a":"<p>Pass a subquery as <code>dbtable</code>, for example <code>\"(SELECT ... WHERE ...) AS t\"</code>, so the database applies the filter and returns only the needed rows. Spark places <code>dbtable</code> in a <code>FROM</code> clause, so the subquery needs an alias. Since Spark 2.4 the <code>query</code> option also exists, but it cannot be combined with <code>partitionColumn</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0076","card":"q-apache-spark-016-4-2-upload-stock-to-the-central-store-pa","sec":"apache-spark-s014","q":"Why is <code>mode(\"append\")</code> risky when you write a daily batch to a Parquet folder, and what are the alternatives?","a":"<p>Running the job again adds the same rows a second time, so downstream totals double. <code>overwrite</code> replaces the target, and with the dynamic partition overwrite mode setting (<code>partitionOverwriteMode=dynamic</code>) it replaces only the partitions being written. <code>errorifexists</code> (the default) and <code>ignore</code> are the other modes. Pick one based on whether the job must be rerunnable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0077","card":"q-apache-spark-016-4-2-upload-stock-to-the-central-store-pa","sec":"apache-spark-s014","q":"What does <code>partitionBy(\"date_col\",\"region_col\")</code> do when writing Parquet, and what is the benefit?","a":"<p>It writes a folder tree <code>date_col=.../region_col=.../part-*.parquet</code>, and the partition column values live in the folder names. Queries that filter on those columns read only matching folders (partition pruning), which cuts I/O. The order of columns sets the folder nesting, so put the most commonly filtered one first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0078","card":"q-apache-spark-016-4-2-upload-stock-to-the-central-store-pa","sec":"apache-spark-s014","q":"What is the risk of <code>partitionBy</code> on a high-cardinality column?","a":"<p>It creates a huge number of small directories and files. That slows down listing and reads, and loads the file system's metadata service. Partition by low-cardinality columns such as date or region, and aim for files of roughly 128 MB or more.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0079","card":"q-apache-spark-016-4-2-upload-stock-to-the-central-store-pa","sec":"apache-spark-s014","q":"What is the difference between <code>partitionBy</code> in <code>df.write</code> and <code>repartition</code> on a DataFrame?","a":"<p><code>partitionBy</code> controls the folder layout on disk when writing. <code>repartition</code> changes how data is split into in-memory partitions across executors, which sets the number of tasks and output files. They are often used together: <code>repartition(\"date_col\").write.partitionBy(\"date_col\")</code> avoids many small files per folder.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0080","card":"q-apache-spark-017-4-2-upload-stock-to-the-central-store-pa","sec":"apache-spark-s014","q":"Why does the code query the minimum and maximum of the ID column before a partitioned JDBC read?","a":"<p>Spark needs <code>lowerBound</code> and <code>upperBound</code> to compute the ranges for each partition. For a filtered subset, the real min and max give evenly sized slices, while hard-coded bounds far wider than the data put most rows into one partition. The upper bound is often set to max plus 1 so the last ID falls inside the final range, although Spark also reads out-of-range rows in the edge partitions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0081","card":"q-apache-spark-017-4-2-upload-stock-to-the-central-store-pa","sec":"apache-spark-s014","q":"What does this skeleton of a Spark JDBC read do, and what happens if you remove <code>numPartitions</code>?","a":"<p>It reads the query result in 4 parallel tasks, each taking a slice of the ID range 1 to 1000 (the bounds set the stride, they do not filter rows). If <code>numPartitions</code> is removed while <code>partitionColumn</code>, <code>lowerBound</code> and <code>upperBound</code> remain, Spark raises an error, because all four options must be set together. With none of them the read is a single partition.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>df = (spark.read.format(&quot;jdbc&quot;)\n      .option(&quot;url&quot;, url)\n      .option(&quot;dbtable&quot;, &quot;(SELECT id, qty FROM items) AS t&quot;)\n      .option(&quot;partitionColumn&quot;, &quot;id&quot;)\n      .option(&quot;lowerBound&quot;, 1)\n      .option(&quot;upperBound&quot;, 1000)\n      .option(&quot;numPartitions&quot;, 4)\n      .load())</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"apache-spark-f0082","card":"q-apache-spark-017-4-2-upload-stock-to-the-central-store-pa","sec":"apache-spark-s014","q":"What does <code>df.show(3)</code> trigger in a Spark program, and why is it a debugging aid but not free?","a":"<p><code>show</code> is an action, so it executes the plan up to that point, reading enough partitions to produce 3 rows. With a long chain, each action may recompute all steps unless data is cached. Use it for inspection, and remove or limit it in production jobs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0083","card":"q-apache-spark-017-4-2-upload-stock-to-the-central-store-pa","sec":"apache-spark-s014","q":"Why does the SQL string in a JDBC read format a date with a function such as <code>date_format(...)</code> before Spark writes it to a partitioned folder?","a":"<p>The partition column's value becomes a folder name, so it should be a clean, predictable string like <code>2024-05-01</code>. A raw timestamp contains spaces and colons that make unusual folder names and finer partitions than wanted. Converting to a date string gives one folder per day.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0084","card":"q-apache-spark-017-4-2-upload-stock-to-the-central-store-pa","sec":"apache-spark-s014","q":"What are the benefits of Parquet over CSV or JSON for intermediate data in Spark?","a":"<p>Parquet is columnar and compressed, stores schema and types, allows reading only needed columns, and supports predicate pushdown through row group statistics. It splits cleanly for parallel reads, and it works as a durable staging area that decouples the extract step from the aggregate step so you can replay aggregation without re-querying source databases. CSV and JSON are text, larger, slower to parse, and need schema inference or declaration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0085","card":"q-apache-spark-018-4-3-aggregate-across-warehouses","sec":"apache-spark-s015","q":"How do you read a whole partitioned Parquet folder in Spark (for example <code>/data/stock/</code>), and how do you read just one day?","a":"<p><code>spark.read.parquet(\"/data/stock/\")</code> reads all files, and Spark discovers the partition columns from the folder names and adds them as columns. To read one day, point at that sub-folder, or filter on the partition column, for example <code>.filter(\"date_col = '2024-05-01'\")</code>, and partition pruning reads only that folder.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0086","card":"q-apache-spark-018-4-3-aggregate-across-warehouses","sec":"apache-spark-s015","q":"How do you write a Spark DataFrame to a relational table, and why is the table name and mode important?","a":"<p>Use <code>df.write.format(\"jdbc\")</code> with <code>url</code>, <code>dbtable</code>, <code>user</code> and <code>password</code>, then <code>save()</code>. The mode decides what happens to existing rows: <code>append</code> adds, <code>overwrite</code> drops and recreates the table (or truncates it with the <code>truncate</code> option). Each executor writes its partitions in parallel, so the table design must allow concurrent inserts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0087","card":"q-apache-spark-018-4-3-aggregate-across-warehouses","sec":"apache-spark-s015","q":"How would you verify that a Spark aggregation matches its inputs?","a":"<p>Pick sample keys and compare the aggregate to totals computed directly from the original databases, and compare row counts and sums of the key metrics between input and output. Reconciliation checks like this catch missed or duplicated records, which are common faults in pipelines.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0088","card":"q-apache-spark-018-4-3-aggregate-across-warehouses","sec":"apache-spark-s015","q":"Why should you delete the intermediate Parquet files after a successful aggregation, and what is the risk of doing it too early?","a":"<p>Pruning prevents the folder from growing forever and prevents the next run from double-reading old data if it reads the whole path. Doing it before the downstream write has succeeded loses your only replay copy. Delete or archive after the result is verified, or use a retention policy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0089","card":"q-apache-spark-019-5-real-time-pipeline-website-analytics","sec":"apache-spark-s016","q":"How would you design a streaming pipeline that needs three outputs from one event stream: windowed summaries in a database, running totals in a cache, and filtered alerts to another topic?","a":"<p>Put events on Kafka, run one Spark Structured Streaming application that reads the topic once into a DataFrame and starts three queries from it: a Kafka sink for the filter, a <code>foreach</code> or <code>foreachBatch</code> writer to Redis for running totals, and a windowed aggregation written to the database. Each query has its own checkpoint. Kafka scales the input, Spark scales processing over executors, Redis scales by sharding, and the database takes parallel writes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0090","card":"q-apache-spark-019-5-real-time-pipeline-website-analytics","sec":"apache-spark-s016","q":"Why put Kafka between the application and Spark rather than sending events directly to Spark?","a":"<p>Kafka decouples the producers from the consumers, buffers bursts, keeps events durable and replayable, and lets several consumers read independently at their own pace. If Spark is down, events wait in Kafka. Replay from stored offsets also supports reprocessing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0091","card":"q-apache-spark-019-5-real-time-pipeline-website-analytics","sec":"apache-spark-s016","q":"Why would you use a Redis sorted set for a running leaderboard of totals?","a":"<p>A sorted set keeps members ordered by a numeric score, with <code>ZINCRBY</code> incrementing a score atomically in O(log N). You can read the top entries by rank or score range at once. It suits live rankings such as top countries by visit time, and it is fast because it lives in memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0092","card":"q-apache-spark-020-5-1-generate-a-visits-stream-code-04-03","sec":"apache-spark-s017","q":"In <code>kafka-python</code>, why do you pass <code>key_serializer</code> and <code>value_serializer</code> to <code>KafkaProducer</code>?","a":"<p>Kafka stores keys and values as bytes. The serializers turn Python objects into bytes, for example <code>str.encode(\"utf-8\")</code> or <code>json.dumps(...).encode()</code>. Without them, the producer rejects a plain string.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0093","card":"q-apache-spark-020-5-1-generate-a-visits-stream-code-04-03","sec":"apache-spark-s017","q":"Why send each record with a key, such as the country, in Kafka?","a":"<p>Records with the same key go to the same partition (the default partitioner hashes the key), and Kafka keeps order within a partition. So all events for a key are processed in order by one consumer task. A null key spreads records across partitions with no ordering by entity. A skewed key can overload one partition.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0094","card":"q-apache-spark-020-5-1-generate-a-visits-stream-code-04-03","sec":"apache-spark-s017","q":"Why must you call <code>flush()</code> before <code>close()</code> on a Kafka producer?","a":"<p><code>send()</code> is asynchronous. It places records in a buffer, and a background thread sends them in batches. <code>flush()</code> blocks until all buffered records are delivered or fail. Closing without it can lose records still in the buffer (<code>close()</code> in kafka-python also flushes in recent versions, but explicit <code>flush()</code> makes the intent clear).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0095","card":"q-apache-spark-020-5-1-generate-a-visits-stream-code-04-03","sec":"apache-spark-s017","q":"How would you confirm that messages reached a Kafka topic from Python, and what does <code>auto_offset_reset=\"latest\"</code> mean for such a test consumer?","a":"<p>Create a <code>KafkaConsumer</code>, <code>subscribe</code> to the topic, and call <code>poll()</code>, then print partition, offset and value. <code>latest</code> means a consumer with no committed offset starts at the end of the topic, so it sees only messages produced after it joins. Use <code>earliest</code> to read what is already there.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0096","card":"q-apache-spark-020-5-1-generate-a-visits-stream-code-04-03","sec":"apache-spark-s017","q":"What does <code>poll()</code> return in <code>kafka-python</code>?","a":"<p>A dictionary that maps each <code>TopicPartition</code> to a list of consumer records that arrived within the timeout. Each record has <code>topic</code>, <code>partition</code>, <code>offset</code>, <code>key</code> and <code>value</code>. It returns an empty dictionary when nothing arrives.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0097","card":"q-apache-spark-021-5-2-streaming-analytics-job-code-04-04","sec":"apache-spark-s018","q":"In Spark Structured Streaming, what does the <code>foreach</code> sink do, and what is wrong with opening a database connection inside the function it calls per row?","a":"<p><code>foreach</code> calls your function for every output row. Opening a connection per row is very slow and can exhaust the database or Redis connection limit. Use a class with <code>open(partition_id, epoch_id)</code>, <code>process(row)</code> and <code>close(error)</code> methods so you connect once per partition per batch, or use <code>foreachBatch</code> and write the whole micro-batch with a bulk writer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0098","card":"q-apache-spark-021-5-2-streaming-analytics-job-code-04-04","sec":"apache-spark-s018","q":"When would you pick <code>foreachBatch</code> over <code>foreach</code> in Structured Streaming?","a":"<p><code>foreachBatch</code> hands you each micro-batch as a regular DataFrame with a batch ID. You can reuse batch writers such as JDBC <code>write</code>, write to several sinks, or run an upsert (<code>MERGE</code>). The batch ID lets you make writes idempotent. <code>foreach</code> is row-level and fits custom per-row logic with no batch writer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0099","card":"q-apache-spark-021-5-2-streaming-analytics-job-code-04-04","sec":"apache-spark-s018","q":"Why does a Kafka message read by Spark need <code>CAST(value AS STRING)</code> and <code>from_json</code> with a schema?","a":"<p>The Kafka source returns <code>key</code> and <code>value</code> as binary columns, along with <code>topic</code>, <code>partition</code>, <code>offset</code> and <code>timestamp</code>. Cast the value to a string, then <code>from_json(col, schema)</code> parses it into a struct, and <code>select(\"col.*\")</code> flattens it. Streaming needs the schema up front, because it cannot infer the schema from data that has not arrived.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0100","card":"q-apache-spark-021-5-2-streaming-analytics-job-code-04-04","sec":"apache-spark-s018","q":"What does <code>startingOffsets</code> control in the Spark Kafka source, and when is it ignored?","a":"<p>It sets where a new query begins: <code>latest</code> (only new data), <code>earliest</code> (the whole topic), or explicit offsets as JSON. It applies only when a query starts without a checkpoint. After a restart with a checkpoint, Spark resumes from the saved offsets and ignores the option.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0101","card":"q-apache-spark-021-5-2-streaming-analytics-job-code-04-04","sec":"apache-spark-s018","q":"Which Kafka package does PySpark need for <code>format(\"kafka\")</code>, and how do you add it?","a":"<p>The <code>spark-sql-kafka-0-10</code> connector, whose Scala suffix and version must match your Spark build, for example <code>org.apache.spark:spark-sql-kafka-0-10_2.12:3.5.1</code> for Spark 3.5.1 on Scala 2.12. Add it with <code>spark.jars.packages</code> or <code>spark-submit --packages</code>. Without it, Spark fails with \"Failed to find data source: kafka\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0102","card":"q-apache-spark-021-5-2-streaming-analytics-job-code-04-04","sec":"apache-spark-s018","q":"What does <code>functions.window(col(\"timestamp\"), \"5 seconds\")</code> do in a streaming <code>groupBy</code>?","a":"<p>It assigns each row to a tumbling 5-second window based on the timestamp column, and aggregation runs per window and per other group key. Adding a slide duration, such as <code>window(col, \"10 seconds\", \"5 seconds\")</code>, makes sliding (overlapping) windows.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0103","card":"q-apache-spark-021-5-2-streaming-analytics-job-code-04-04","sec":"apache-spark-s018","q":"Why does a windowed aggregation need <code>withWatermark</code> before <code>groupBy</code> in append mode?","a":"<p>In append mode, Spark emits a window only when it is final, which it knows only once the watermark has passed the window end. <code>withWatermark</code> must be applied to the same timestamp column used in <code>window</code>, before the aggregation. Without it, Spark has no way to finalize windows and the query fails in append mode or keeps unbounded state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0104","card":"q-apache-spark-021-5-2-streaming-analytics-job-code-04-04","sec":"apache-spark-s018","q":"This code adds a watermark on a column created with <code>current_timestamp()</code>. What does that mean for the window semantics?","a":"<p>The window is based on the time Spark processes the row, not the time the event happened, so it is processing-time windowing. It is simple and never sees late data, but results depend on arrival timing. For correct event-time analysis, use the timestamp inside the event, such as a parsed <code>visit_date</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>(visits_df\n  .withColumn(&quot;timestamp&quot;, functions.current_timestamp())\n  .withWatermark(&quot;timestamp&quot;, &quot;5 seconds&quot;))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"apache-spark-f0105","card":"q-apache-spark-022-5-2-streaming-analytics-job-code-04-04","sec":"apache-spark-s018","q":"In PySpark, why does calling <code>.start()</code> on three queries from the same DataFrame create three independent jobs?","a":"<p>Each <code>writeStream...start()</code> creates its own streaming query with its own checkpoint, trigger and state. They run concurrently, and each reads the Kafka source on its own, so the topic is consumed three times. If that matters for load, combine the outputs in one <code>foreachBatch</code>, which sees each micro-batch once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0106","card":"q-apache-spark-022-5-2-streaming-analytics-job-code-04-04","sec":"apache-spark-s018","q":"What is the difference between <code>query.awaitTermination()</code> and <code>spark.streams.awaitAnyTermination()</code>?","a":"<p><code>awaitTermination()</code> blocks on one query. When several queries run, calling it on the last one makes the program wait only for that one, and an error in another query may go unnoticed. <code>spark.streams.awaitAnyTermination()</code> blocks until any active query stops or fails, which is safer for multi-query applications.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0107","card":"q-apache-spark-022-5-2-streaming-analytics-job-code-04-04","sec":"apache-spark-s018","q":"Why does a Kafka sink in Structured Streaming require a <code>checkpointLocation</code>?","a":"<p>The checkpoint records which micro-batches have been processed so Spark can recover without losing or repeating them. The Kafka sink query fails to start without one (only console and memory sinks get a temporary one automatically). Put it on durable storage and use a distinct path per query.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0108","card":"q-apache-spark-022-5-2-streaming-analytics-job-code-04-04","sec":"apache-spark-s018","q":"How would you write a filtered stream back to a Kafka topic in PySpark? Which columns does the Kafka sink need?","a":"<p>Select a <code>value</code> column (string or binary), plus an optional <code>key</code>, then <code>writeStream.format(\"kafka\")</code> with <code>kafka.bootstrap.servers</code>, <code>topic</code> and <code>checkpointLocation</code>, then <code>start()</code>. <code>value</code> is required. If you do not set the <code>topic</code> option, a <code>topic</code> column must exist in the data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0109","card":"q-apache-spark-022-5-2-streaming-analytics-job-code-04-04","sec":"apache-spark-s018","q":"Why use <code>format_string</code> or <code>to_json(struct(...))</code> to build the Kafka value, and which is better for downstream consumers?","a":"<p>The sink needs a single <code>value</code> column. <code>format_string</code> builds a delimited text line, which is fragile when fields contain the delimiter or the order changes. <code>to_json(struct(...))</code> gives self-describing JSON that consumers parse by field name, so it is usually the better choice, and a schema registry with Avro is better still for contracts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0110","card":"q-apache-spark-022-5-2-streaming-analytics-job-code-04-04","sec":"apache-spark-s018","q":"What happens to the Redis and MariaDB writes in a streaming job if the job restarts and replays the last micro-batch?","a":"<p>A replayed batch writes again. An insert into MariaDB creates duplicate rows, and <code>ZINCRBY</code> in Redis counts the records twice. Make the writers idempotent, for example an upsert keyed on window and action, or store the processed batch ID and skip a batch already written.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0111","card":"q-apache-spark-022-5-2-streaming-analytics-job-code-04-04","sec":"apache-spark-s018","q":"How do you stop a running streaming query gracefully?","a":"<p>Call <code>query.stop()</code> on the <code>StreamingQuery</code> handle (or <code>spark.streams.active</code> to find it). Spark finishes the current micro-batch where possible, and offsets are saved in the checkpoint, so the next start resumes from there. Killing the process abruptly also recovers from the checkpoint but may repeat the last batch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0112","card":"q-apache-spark-023-5-3-execute-and-monitor-code-04-05","sec":"apache-spark-s019","q":"When you run a Kafka consumer, a streaming job and a producer for a test, in which order should you start them and why?","a":"<p>Start the monitoring consumer first, then the streaming job, then the producer. A consumer that uses <code>latest</code> sees only messages produced after it starts, and a Spark query starting with <code>latest</code> also misses earlier events. Starting consumers first ensures nothing is skipped.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0113","card":"q-apache-spark-023-5-3-execute-and-monitor-code-04-05","sec":"apache-spark-s019","q":"In <code>kafka-python</code>, what does <code>group_id=None</code> mean for a consumer?","a":"<p>The consumer does not join a consumer group and does not commit offsets, so it does not share partitions with other consumers and does not remember its position. It is suitable for a monitoring tool that wants to read independently of any other consumer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0114","card":"q-apache-spark-023-5-3-execute-and-monitor-code-04-05","sec":"apache-spark-s019","q":"How can you monitor a running Spark Structured Streaming query?","a":"<p>Use the <code>StreamingQuery</code> object: <code>query.status</code>, <code>query.lastProgress</code> and <code>query.recentProgress</code> show input rate, processing rate, batch duration, state size and offsets. The Spark UI has a Structured Streaming tab with the same, and you can attach a <code>StreamingQueryListener</code>. If input rate exceeds processing rate, the job falls behind.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0115","card":"q-apache-spark-023-5-3-execute-and-monitor-code-04-05","sec":"apache-spark-s019","q":"Your streaming job is running but a downstream Redis total does not grow. What do you check?","a":"<p>First check the query is active and has no exception (<code>query.exception()</code>, <code>query.status</code>). Then see whether the input rate is greater than zero in <code>lastProgress</code>, whether the Kafka offsets are moving, and whether <code>startingOffsets</code> skipped the data. Then check the writer function for errors (they appear in executor logs), connection settings and whether the filter dropped every row.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0116","card":"q-apache-spark-023-5-3-execute-and-monitor-code-04-05","sec":"apache-spark-s019","q":"What does <code>ZREVRANGEBYSCORE key max min WITHSCORES</code> return in Redis, and is it current?","a":"<p>It returns members with scores between min and max, from highest to lowest, with their scores. As of Redis 6.2 it is deprecated in favor of <code>ZRANGE key max min BYSCORE REV WITHSCORES</code>. It still works, but new code should use <code>ZRANGE</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0117","card":"q-apache-spark-024-scaling-processing","sec":"apache-spark-s020","q":"What are the main ways to scale a Spark processing job?","a":"<p>Filter and project columns early to shrink data, run narrow transformations before wide ones, reduce late, repartition to a sensible number of partitions, cache reused intermediate results, and avoid sending data to the driver. Beyond code, add executors or cores, and fix skew.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0118","card":"q-apache-spark-024-scaling-processing","sec":"apache-spark-s020","q":"What do <code>repartition</code> and <code>coalesce</code> do, and when do you choose each?","a":"<p><code>repartition(n)</code> does a full shuffle and can increase or decrease partitions with even sizes. <code>coalesce(n)</code> only merges existing partitions without a full shuffle, so it is cheaper but can only decrease the count and may leave sizes uneven. Use <code>coalesce</code> to reduce file counts before a write, and <code>repartition</code> to increase parallelism or balance data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0119","card":"q-apache-spark-024-scaling-processing","sec":"apache-spark-s020","q":"When does <code>cache()</code> or <code>persist()</code> help in Spark, and what is the risk?","a":"<p>It helps when the same DataFrame is used by more than one action, because Spark otherwise recomputes it from the source each time. The risks are memory pressure and evicted partitions, and that caching a DataFrame used once only wastes memory. Call <code>unpersist()</code> when finished. <code>persist</code> lets you pick a storage level such as <code>MEMORY_AND_DISK</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0120","card":"q-apache-spark-024-scaling-processing","sec":"apache-spark-s020","q":"What is data skew in Spark and how do you detect and fix it?","a":"<p>Skew is when a few keys hold most rows, so one task runs far longer than the rest in a stage. You see it in the Spark UI as a few tasks with huge shuffle read or duration. Fixes include salting the hot key, using a broadcast join for a small side, and enabling adaptive query execution skew-join handling (on by default for Spark 3.2 and later through <code>spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0121","card":"q-apache-spark-024-scaling-processing","sec":"apache-spark-s020","q":"What is a broadcast join, and when is it better than a shuffle join?","a":"<p>Spark sends a copy of the small table to every executor, so the large table is joined locally and not shuffled. It is best when one side fits easily in executor memory. Spark does it automatically when the table is smaller than <code>spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold</code> (10 MB by default), or you can hint it with <code>broadcast(df)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0122","card":"q-apache-spark-024-scaling-processing","sec":"apache-spark-s020","q":"Why can too many partitions or too few partitions both hurt a Spark job?","a":"<p>Too few partitions means less parallelism and large tasks that may spill or run out of memory. Too many means tiny tasks where scheduling overhead dominates, and many small output files. A common guide is a few partitions per CPU core and partitions of about 100-200 MB.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0123","card":"q-apache-spark-025-building-resiliency","sec":"apache-spark-s021","q":"How does Spark recover from failures during a batch job?","a":"<p>Spark records the lineage of each partition. If a task fails, it is retried (up to <code>spark.task.maxFailures</code>, default 4 on a cluster), and if an executor is lost its lost partitions are recomputed from lineage on another executor. Lost shuffle files cause the earlier stage to rerun. A driver failure ends the application, so run it under a supervisor or a cluster manager that restarts it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0124","card":"q-apache-spark-025-building-resiliency","sec":"apache-spark-s021","q":"Why is Spark's fault tolerance not enough to make a whole pipeline resilient?","a":"<p>Spark protects only its own computation. The inputs and outputs (Kafka, databases, file systems) must also be redundant and durable, the cluster should have no single node of failure, and you need monitoring and alerting for jobs that fail. Writes to external systems may also duplicate on retry, so sinks should be idempotent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0125","card":"q-apache-spark-025-building-resiliency","sec":"apache-spark-s021","q":"What do you need so that a Spark Structured Streaming job survives a restart?","a":"<p>A checkpoint location on durable shared storage, a replayable source such as Kafka whose offsets are tracked in the checkpoint, and an automatic restart mechanism (cluster manager, Kubernetes restart policy or an orchestrator). With these, the job resumes from the last committed batch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0126","card":"q-apache-spark-025-building-resiliency","sec":"apache-spark-s021","q":"What does speculative execution do in Spark and what is its risk?","a":"<p>With <code>spark.speculation</code> enabled, Spark launches a duplicate of a slow-running task on another executor and takes the first to finish. It helps with a slow node. The risk is that side effects such as writes to an external database may run twice, so it is risky with non-idempotent sinks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0127","card":"q-apache-spark-025-building-resiliency","sec":"apache-spark-s021","q":"How can a staging table make a Spark load into a relational database safer than writing straight into the target table?","a":"<p>Write the new rows to a staging table first, then merge or swap them into the target in one database transaction. If the Spark job fails halfway, the target is untouched and you drop or truncate the staging table and rerun. The cost is extra storage and one more step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0128","card":"q-apache-spark-025-building-resiliency","sec":"apache-spark-s021","q":"What should you monitor and alert on for a production Spark pipeline?","a":"<p>Job and stage failures, batch duration or lag (input rate versus processing rate for streaming), Kafka consumer lag, executor loss and memory spills, data quality counts (rows in versus rows out), and freshness of the output. Alert on unhandled failures so that someone is paged.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0129","card":"q-apache-spark-026-batch-vs-real-time-choosing","sec":"apache-spark-s022","q":"What makes real-time pipelines harder to get right than batch?","a":"<p>The input is unbounded, so memory and cluster size are hard to plan. Watermarks and windows can drop late events and produce wrong aggregates. State must stay correct across the cluster and after failures. Reprocessing is not a plain rerun, because checkpoints, state and offsets need resetting. Operations and testing are also more complex.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0130","card":"q-apache-spark-026-batch-vs-real-time-choosing","sec":"apache-spark-s022","q":"When is batch the better choice over streaming?","a":"<p>When minutes or hours of latency are fine, when you run heavy aggregation or analytics over large history, or when you need simple implementation, easy debugging and straightforward reprocessing. It is also cheaper, because the cluster can be released after the run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0131","card":"q-apache-spark-026-batch-vs-real-time-choosing","sec":"apache-spark-s022","q":"When is streaming the better choice?","a":"<p>When actions must happen within seconds, the input is a continuous stream, the processing needs few steps (more steps mean more state and watermark complexity), and you have the compute to run it continuously.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0132","card":"q-apache-spark-026-batch-vs-real-time-choosing","sec":"apache-spark-s022","q":"How can Spark give streaming-style incremental processing at batch-style cost?","a":"<p>Use a trigger such as <code>Trigger.AvailableNow</code> (Spark 3.3 and later; in PySpark <code>trigger(availableNow=True)</code>). The query processes all data currently available, in micro-batches, and then stops. You keep checkpoints and incremental offsets, but you pay for compute only while it runs, which suits hourly or daily incremental loads.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0133","card":"q-apache-spark-026-batch-vs-real-time-choosing","sec":"apache-spark-s022","q":"What are the Lambda and Kappa architectures, and how do they relate to this choice?","a":"<p>Lambda keeps a batch layer for complete, accurate results and a speed layer for low-latency approximate results, then merges them, which means two code paths. Kappa uses only a streaming system and replays the log for reprocessing, with one code path. Spark's unified batch and streaming API reduces the cost of Lambda's duplicate logic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0134","card":"q-apache-spark-027-scaling-extraction-and-loading","sec":"apache-spark-s023","q":"Why are extraction and loading often the bottleneck in a Spark pipeline?","a":"<p>They are disk and network bound and depend on external systems that may not scale like Spark. A fast cluster waits on a slow single-threaded read from a database or a write to a sink with limited connections. Every stage must scale, so design reads and writes for parallelism first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0135","card":"q-apache-spark-027-scaling-extraction-and-loading","sec":"apache-spark-s023","q":"What are the points to consider when scaling parallel writes into a database sink?","a":"<p>Each executor writes independently, so check how the sink handles concurrent transactions, locking and ordering. Cap the number of parallel connections to what the database supports, using <code>numPartitions</code> or <code>coalesce</code>. Batch inserts, and defend against duplicate and missing updates when tasks retry.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0136","card":"q-apache-spark-027-scaling-extraction-and-loading","sec":"apache-spark-s023","q":"How do you keep a Spark batch read from a production database and a Kafka backlog from overloading the source or the job?","a":"<p>For a database, read from a replica so production traffic is not affected. For Kafka, set <code>maxOffsetsPerTrigger</code> to limit the size of each micro-batch, so the job does not fall behind or run out of memory after a backlog builds up.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0137","card":"q-apache-spark-027-scaling-extraction-and-loading","sec":"apache-spark-s023","q":"Why must extraction defend against missed and duplicate records?","a":"<p>Retries, restarts and overlapping time windows can read a row twice or skip one. Use stable bounds, track the high-water mark (the last ID or timestamp processed), and deduplicate by a unique key downstream. Without that, totals drift in ways that are hard to notice.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0138","card":"q-apache-spark-028-7-hybrid-pipeline-exercise","sec":"apache-spark-s024","q":"How would you design a pipeline that daily extracts important records from a database, sends them to Kafka, and keeps live counters per category?","a":"<p>A batch Spark job reads the records through a partitioned, filtered JDBC read and writes them to a Kafka topic. A streaming Spark job reads that topic and increments a counter per category in a Redis sorted set. The topic decouples the two jobs, so each can be scheduled, scaled and restarted on its own.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0139","card":"q-apache-spark-029-7-1-job-1-extract-long-last-actions-batc","sec":"apache-spark-s025","q":"Why compute the date range in Python and put it in the SQL, rather than reading the whole table into Spark and filtering?","a":"<p>The filter is applied inside the database as part of the subquery, so only matching rows cross the network and the database can use an index on the date column. Filtering in Spark after a full read moves every row first. Spark can push simple filters down by itself, but an explicit subquery guarantees it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0140","card":"q-apache-spark-029-7-1-job-1-extract-long-last-actions-batc","sec":"apache-spark-s025","q":"What is the pitfall in a JDBC bounds query using <code>min(ID)</code> and <code>max(ID)</code> when the filter matches no rows?","a":"<p>Both return <code>NULL</code>, which arrives in Python as <code>None</code>, so <code>max + 1</code> raises a <code>TypeError</code> and the Spark read gets invalid bounds. Check for an empty result and exit or skip the job before building the read.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0141","card":"q-apache-spark-029-7-1-job-1-extract-long-last-actions-batc","sec":"apache-spark-s025","q":"Why filter with <code>BETWEEN '&lt;start&gt;' AND '&lt;end&gt;'</code> on a date column carefully when the column is a datetime?","a":"<p><code>BETWEEN</code> is inclusive on both ends, and a bare date string is read as midnight. With a datetime column, a range ending at a midnight timestamp includes rows exactly at that midnight, which belong to the next day. Prefer a half-open range: <code>ts &gt;= start AND ts &lt; next_day</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0142","card":"q-apache-spark-030-7-1-job-1-extract-long-last-actions-batc","sec":"apache-spark-s025","q":"How do you write a batch DataFrame to Kafka in PySpark, and what columns does it need?","a":"<p>Use <code>df.write.format(\"kafka\")</code> with <code>kafka.bootstrap.servers</code> and <code>topic</code>, then <code>save()</code>. The DataFrame must have a <code>value</code> column of string or binary type. A <code>key</code> column is optional and decides the partition, and a <code>topic</code> column can replace the <code>topic</code> option. Use <code>selectExpr(\"... as key\", \"... as value\")</code> to build them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0143","card":"q-apache-spark-030-7-1-job-1-extract-long-last-actions-batc","sec":"apache-spark-s025","q":"What is the effect of using the same field as the Kafka key and value in a batch write?","a":"<p>The key sends all records of one category to the same partition, which keeps order per category. The value carries the content that consumers read. Using one field for both is fine when the value is just the category label, but richer messages should carry JSON in the value.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0144","card":"q-apache-spark-030-7-1-job-1-extract-long-last-actions-batc","sec":"apache-spark-s025","q":"Does a batch Kafka write guarantee no duplicates?","a":"<p>No. The Kafka sink is at-least-once. If a task fails and retries, records can be written more than once. Consumers that need exactly-once results must deduplicate or use idempotent logic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0145","card":"q-apache-spark-031-7-2-job-2-scorecard-streaming-code-06-04","sec":"apache-spark-s026","q":"Why does a streaming job that reads a single plain-text field from Kafka not need <code>from_json</code> or a schema?","a":"<p>The Kafka <code>value</code> is a binary column. If the message is a simple string, <code>CAST(value AS STRING)</code> gives the data directly. A schema is needed only when the value holds structured data such as JSON that must be split into fields.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"apache-spark-f0146","card":"q-apache-spark-031-7-2-job-2-scorecard-streaming-code-06-04","sec":"apache-spark-s026","q":"Why does the monitoring loop in a streaming program run after <code>start()</code> rather than call <code>awaitTermination()</code> first?","a":"<p><code>start()</code> returns at once and the query runs in background threads, so the main thread is free to do other work such as polling Redis. <code>awaitTermination()</code> blocks the main thread, so any code placed after it does not run until the query stops.</p>","hand":true}];
